<div class="editorial"><header class="editorial-hero"><span class="eyebrow">Explore. Understand. Share.</span><h1>One Map.<br>Countless Experiences.<br>Shared Awareness.</h1><p class="lead">Explore the map. Understand the reports. Share what matters.</p><p>HerMap brings community-reported experiences together to help women stay informed about the places around them.</p><div class="cta-row"><a class="button button-primary" href="<?= ps_escape(ps_url()) ?>">Explore the map <?= ps_icon('map') ?></a><a class="button button-secondary" href="<?= ps_escape(ps_url('#report')) ?>">Report an incident <?= ps_icon('plus') ?></a></div></header>
<div class="steps">
<section class="how-step"><span class="step-number">01</span><div><h2>Explore a location</h2><p>Start with India. Search a PIN, post office or district, or zoom and tap an area. State names and boundaries help you find your bearings; the island shortcuts bring Lakshadweep and Andaman &amp; Nicobar into view.</p></div><div class="step-art" aria-hidden="true"><?= ps_icon('map-location-dot') ?><span>India → your broad area</span></div></section>
<section class="how-step"><span class="step-number">02</span><div><h2>Understand the colours</h2><p>Darker coral shades show higher recorded reporting activity in the selected window. Grey indicates insufficient information. Colours reflect reports, not verified crime rates or a guarantee of safety.</p></div><div class="step-art" aria-hidden="true"><span class="legend-scale"></span><span>Lower → higher activity</span></div></section>
<section class="how-step"><span class="step-number">03</span><div><h2>Share an experience</h2><p>Choose a category, a broad location and the incident date and time. No public profile is required. Optional venue or online account details stay encrypted for private review. Avoid survivor identities, real names, contact details and children’s information.</p></div><div class="step-art" aria-hidden="true"><?= ps_icon('pen-to-square') ?><span>Structured report · no public profile</span></div></section>
<section class="how-step"><span class="step-number">04</span><div><h2>Reports become community awareness</h2><p>Submissions enter a private queue for checks on privacy, duplicates and abuse. Eligible reports contribute to delayed, grouped statistics. Personal stories and account details are never displayed on the map. Review does not independently verify an incident.</p></div><div class="step-art" aria-hidden="true"><?= ps_icon('user-shield') ?><span>Private review → broad patterns</span></div></section>
<section class="how-step"><span class="step-number">05</span><div><h2>Make more informed decisions</h2><p>Consider these patterns alongside your surroundings, trusted local advice and official safety information. Community reports can be incomplete. Missing data does not mean an area is safe.</p></div><div class="step-art" aria-hidden="true"><?= ps_icon('hand-holding-heart') ?><span>A little awareness. A more informed journey.</span></div></section>
</div><section class="editorial-cta"><h2>Your Voice Can Make a Difference.</h2><p>A shared experience can become useful knowledge for someone else. Know Before You Go.</p><div class="cta-row"><a class="button button-primary" href="<?= ps_escape(ps_url('#report')) ?>">Report an incident <?= ps_icon('plus') ?></a><a class="button button-secondary" href="<?= ps_escape(ps_url()) ?>">Explore the map <?= ps_icon('arrow-right') ?></a></div><p class="emergency-note">HerMap is an awareness platform. In an emergency in India, <a href="tel:112">call 112</a>.</p></section>
<details class="method-details"><summary>Data, map colours and privacy safeguards</summary>
<h2>Private review before publication</h2><p>Every submission enters a private queue. Moderators check privacy, duplicate concerns and abuse; approval does not establish that an incident happened. Optional venue names and websites stay private and are not used to publish accusations or business ratings.</p>
<h2>Broad, delayed totals</h2><p>The map groups reports into 0.25° longitude/latitude cells, approximately 20–28 km across in India. The selected PIN centre assigns a cell by default. An optional explicitly shared point can assign the cell instead; it must be within 75 km of the selected approximate PIN centre. Its exact coordinates are encrypted for private review and never public. Only approved reports at least <?= PUBLICATION_DELAY_HOURS ?> hours past approval enter summaries. Counts below <?= PUBLIC_MIN_REPORTS ?> are withheld; eligible numbers are banded in groups of five, such as 5–9.</p><p>The reporting window follows the incident date, in IST. Category, hour-of-day and weekday patterns each need at least <?= PUBLIC_MIN_REPORTS ?> eligible reports in that group. Ties currently show the first tied category/hour/day in a consistent order, not proof of a unique peak. Hour labels such as 9 pm–10 pm IST combine many dates; they are not an individual incident timestamp.</p>
<h2>Click and explore</h2><p>Tap inside India or select a searched post office to load a broad cell summary. Coloured circles represent coarse cell centres with enough eligible reports. Coral bands correspond to 5–9, 10–19, 20–29 and 30+ eligible reports in each cell, subject to the configured minimum. More intense colour means more reporting activity in the selected window, not greater danger. No reports or a withheld group does not mean an area is safe. Counts can change after review, removal, expiry or a change of reporting window.</p>
<h2>PIN and place data</h2><p>The location database combines the supplied country/state/city tables, the historical <a href="https://github.com/dpnkrpl/indian-pincodes-database/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Indian PIN directory</a> and <a href="https://www.geonames.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GeoNames</a> postal data. GeoNames coordinates are approximate PIN centres, not post-office entrances or PIN polygons. A PIN serves multiple offices and historical entries may be outdated. Entries without a usable coordinate cannot be used to submit a mapped report. No claim of complete or current India Post coverage is made.</p>
<h2>India geography</h2><p>The displayed outline comes from the <a href="https://surveyofindia.gov.in/pages/outline-maps-of-india" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Survey of India international-boundary vector download</a>. It includes northern territory in that official outline and the island groups. It has been transformed to geographic coordinates and lightly simplified for web display. The source is generalised at 1:16 million scale, suitable for an overview, not surveying, navigation or precise jurisdiction decisions. State and Union Territory boundaries are from the Survey of India dataset published by the National Water Data Portal (NWIC). They are transformed and generalised for display, with names normalised and coastlines clipped to the official outline. Small island groups have labelled zoom shortcuts. Attribution and source-use conditions are retained. HerMap is not endorsed by Survey of India.</p>
<h2>Community counters and graphs</h2><p>Public report totals mean submissions received, including unreviewed or later-deleted reports; they are distinct from eligible map counts. Visits count valid public-page loads, including reloads and tabs; admin and API requests are excluded. Active visits are page tokens active in the last two minutes, not unique people. Bot visits can affect counts. Admin report graphs use submission date in IST; map time/day patterns use the reported incident date and time.</p>
<h2>Support and corrections</h2><p>HerMap cannot dispatch help or file a police complaint. In immediate danger in India, <a href="tel:112">call 112</a>. Send privacy, geography, accuracy or misuse concerns to <a href="mailto:<?= ps_escape(SUPPORT_EMAIL) ?>"><?= ps_escape(SUPPORT_EMAIL) ?></a>, without identifying survivors or children. Repeated reports do not prove a venue unsafe.</p>
</details></div>
